// The AI orchestration layer: the provider-agnostic tool-calling loop. The single seam between the
// loop and a concrete model API is ONE request — `infer_step` — so a provider is a module that serves
// it (`use gemini.provider(...)` / `use openai.provider(...)` at the app root) and this module never
// names a provider. Adding a provider is adding a module; nothing here changes.
//
// Everything else is plain Katari over the agnostic vocabulary in `ai.types`: derive each
// tool's schema with `reflection.get_metadata`, ask the ambient provider for a step, dispatch tool
// calls concurrently with `parallel for` (each validated against its tool's schema by
// `reflection.call_agent` — an `as_tool` tool from an MCP server validates against the server's
// schema the same way), and feed the results back until the model answers.
//
// Files flow through the loop as blob handles: a tool result's `file` values are collected off the
// result's wire form (`collect_result_files`) and ride the `tool_result`, so the provider can inline
// their content for the model while the result is fresh — and the model can replay any handle it has
// seen into a later tool call (`view_image` re-reads one on demand).

import ai.types

@"One model step over the conversation + tool schemas — THE provider seam. A provider module serves
this request for the extent of a continuation (`use gemini.provider(...)`), so the loop stays
provider-agnostic and providers stay loop-agnostic. The reply is an OUTCOME sum, never a throw: on
success `inferred(result)` carries the step's decision AND its token accounting (`types.step_result`,
so any loop over this seam can meter its context for free); on failure `inference_failed(error)`
carries the `step_error` the provider gave up on (its retries exhausted, or a fatal status). Handing the
failure back as a VALUE — rather than throwing from the provider's DEEP handler — is what lets each loop
decide at its own PERFORM site whether to absorb the failure (the region loop abandons just that turn and
lives on) or re-raise it into its own caller (the one-shot loops): a deep handler can only throw OUT of
its own context, never INTO the loop's, so the resume value is the only channel that reaches the loop."
request infer_step(history: array[types.message], tool_metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> step_outcome

// The error surface of one model step: malformed provider JSON (`json.parse_error`), a non-2xx API
// status (`http.status_error`), or a transport failure (`http.fetch_error`). A provider no longer THROWS
// this — it hands it back as `inference_failed(error)` (see `infer_step`), so the failure surfaces at the
// loop's perform site, not the provider's deep use site. A provider builds its request body as a raw value
// tree and posts it with `http.post_json`, which parses the reply — so the only JSON failure a step can
// carry is that parse. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type step_error = json.parse_error | http.status_error | http.fetch_error

@"One model step's OUTCOME success: the provider produced a step (`types.step_result` for `infer_step`,
`types.object_result` for `infer_object`). Generic over the payload so both seam requests share one sum shape."
data inferred[T](@"The step the provider produced." result: T)

@"One model step's OUTCOME failure: the provider gave up — a transient error past its retry budget, or a
fatal status / malformed body. Carried back as a VALUE so a loop handles it at its perform site, rather than
the provider throwing out of its own deep handler and unwinding the whole loop with it."
data inference_failed(@"The step error the provider surfaced." error: step_error)

// The outcome of one `infer_step`: the produced step, or the provider's surfaced failure. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type step_outcome = inferred[types.step_result] | inference_failed

// The outcome of one `infer_object`: the produced object, or the provider's surfaced failure. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type object_outcome = inferred[types.object_result] | inference_failed

@"One structured-output step over the conversation — the seam's second request, served by the same
provider handler as `infer_step`. @schema@ is the JSON Schema the reply must conform to (each
provider maps it onto its native constrained decoding); the reply is an OUTCOME — `inferred(result)`
with the decoded object and usage, or `inference_failed(error)`. Prefer `infer_structured[T]`, which
reifies T's schema, validates the reply, and re-raises a failure at your own call site."
request infer_object(history: array[types.message], schema: unknown) -> object_outcome

@"Infer a single reply with no tools (the common chat case). A provider failure re-raises here as
`prelude.throw[step_error]` — this is a one-shot call, so a caller that wants to survive a failed step
installs its own handler (the region loop does not use this; it drives the seam directly and absorbs)."
agent reply(history: array[types.message]) -> string {
  match (infer_step(history = history, tool_metas = [])) {
    case inferred(result => result) -> {
      match (result.step) {
        case types.step_final(text => text) -> text
        case types.step_call(calls => _) -> "(unexpected tool call without tools)"
      }
    }
    case inference_failed(error => error) -> prelude.throw(error = error)
  }
}

@"Infer a single reply as a typed value — structured output with the type as the contract:
`reflection.schema_of[T]` reifies T's schema, the ambient provider decodes against it natively
(Gemini's responseSchema, OpenAI's response_format, Anthropic a forced tool), and the decoded object
validates as T (`json.validate` — a nonconforming reply throws `json.validation_error`). Instantiate
T explicitly; it appears only in the result, so it is never inferable. An object type
(`type shape = { ... }`) makes the friendliest model-facing schema — a data type asks the model to
produce its `$katari_constructor` wrapper too."
agent infer_structured[T](history: array[types.message]) -> T {
  match (infer_object(history = history, schema = reflection.schema_of[T]())) {
    case inferred(result => result) -> json.validate[T](value = result.value)
    case inference_failed(error => error) -> prelude.throw(error = error)
  }
}

// ── transient retry: the provider layer's resilience ──────────────────────────
//
// A provider's `infer_step` / `infer_object` handler runs at the app root (its `use` site), ABOVE the
// loop — so a step failure (`step_error`) surfaces THERE and the tool loop cannot catch it (the loop is the
// handler's continuation, not an enclosing handler). A transient failure — a 429 rate limit, a 5xx, a
// dropped connection — would therefore end a long-lived bot. The fix lives where the error IS catchable:
// inside the provider's own handler body, whose effect row is CONCRETE, so a `prelude.throw[step_error]`
// handler discharges it (the same shape as an app's resilient `try_send`). Each provider retries a
// transient step with exponential backoff; these two pure helpers make the decision, and the retry loop
// itself lives in each handler body — a callback-wrapped `retry(task)` would commonise the whole thing,
// which is not the point (the decision is the shared part, not the control flow).

@"Whether a step error is TRANSIENT — worth a backoff and a retry — as opposed to fatal. A transport
failure (`http.fetch_error`: DNS, a refused connection, a timeout, a mid-flight restart) is always
transient; a status error is transient for 408 (request timeout), 429 (rate limit) and any 5xx
(server-side), and fatal for every other status (401/403/404 … — a retry will not fix them); a
`json.parse_error` is fatal (a malformed body will not parse on a retry)."
agent is_transient(error: step_error) -> boolean {
  match (error) {
    case http.fetch_error(message => _) -> true
    case http.status_error(status => status, body => _) -> status == 408 || status == 429 || status >= 500
    case json.parse_error(message => _) -> false
  }
}

@"The backoff delay in milliseconds before retry @attempt@ (0-based): @base_ms@ doubled per attempt (base,
2×base, 4×base, …) and capped at one minute, so a long outage does not sleep unboundedly between the final
tries."
agent backoff_ms(attempt: integer, base_ms: number) -> number {
  let grown = for (let _step in array.range(start = 0, end = attempt), var delay: number = base_ms) {
    next with { delay = delay * 2.0 }
  } then (_steps) { delay }
  math.min(left = grown, right = 60000.0)
}

@"The outcome of ONE provider step attempt, threaded back to a provider's retry loop: the step's @result@
on success. A provider's per-attempt helper catches a `step_error` into `attempt_err` so the loop can
classify it (`is_transient`) and either back off and retry or propagate."
data attempt_ok[T](result: T)

@"The outcome of ONE provider step attempt: the @error@ the attempt caught, for the retry loop to classify."
data attempt_err(error: step_error)

// A step attempt's verdict: the result, or the caught error to classify and maybe retry. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type attempt[T] = attempt_ok[T] | attempt_err

@"Two tools in one loop's tool list share a metadata name, so the model's pick could not be routed
unambiguously (dispatch matches by name — the first match would silently win, and the other tool
would be unreachable). A configuration defect the app fixes — a `prefix` on one of the colliding MCP
connections, or a rename — typed so a supervisor reports it instead of the run panicking."
data duplicate_tool(@"The name two tools share." name: string)

@"Validate one step's assembled tool metadata: every name unique, or the typed `duplicate_tool`.
Every loop runs its tool list through this once per turn, so a colliding list fails loudly at the
first step instead of silently routing every pick to whichever tool sorts first."
agent ensure_unique_names(metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> array[reflection.agent_metadata] {
  for (let meta in metas, var seen: record[boolean] = record.empty()) {
    if (record.has(target = seen, key = meta.name)) {
      prelude.throw(error = duplicate_tool(name = meta.name))
    } else {
      next with { seen = record.set(target = seen, key = meta.name, value = true) }
    }
  } then (_metas) { null }
  metas
}

@"Tool: load an image file into your visual context. Pass the file's handle object — a bare
{\"$katari_ref\": \"<id>\"} suffices (copy the id from the conversation); the image content arrives with
this tool's result, so you can see it on your next step."
agent view_image(image: file) -> file {
  image
}

@"Run the tool-using model loop, generic over the tools' shared effect @E@: derive each tool's schema
with `reflection.get_metadata`, then up to @max_steps@ times perform `infer_step` against the ambient
provider; on a batch of tool calls, dispatch them all CONCURRENTLY with `parallel for` (each resolves
its tool by name and invokes it by value via `reflection.call_agent`, validating the model's args
against the tool's schema), then feed every result back as a new turn; on a final answer, return it."
agent infer_with_tools[effect E](
  history: array[types.message],
  tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  max_steps: integer,
) -> string {
  // The one turn loop lives in `session_turn_loop`; this one-shot shape is that loop minus the
  // conversation and the usage figure it threads (a caller who wants those calls it directly).
  session_turn_loop[E](conversation = history, tools = tools, max_steps = max_steps).reply
}

@"The forced tool-less close every loop plays when its step budget runs out: a user turn telling the
model the budget is spent, so ONE last step commits to an answer from what the tools already gathered
instead of discarding it."
agent budget_exhausted_turn() -> types.message {
  types.turn(
    role = "user",
    text = "You have used your entire tool-call budget. Do not call any more tools. Answer the original question now, as best you can, using only what you have already gathered.",
    files = [],
  )
}

@"Find the tool whose metadata name is the model's pick and invoke it by value via
`reflection.call_agent` (the model's args ride as a plain value, and the delegation boundary
validates them against the tool's schema — the compiled signature of a Katari agent, or the
server-declared schema of an MCP tool — while the boundary's unconditional wire→value conversion turns
any replayed `$katari_ref` into the `file` it denotes). Every way a call can go wrong is fed back to the
model as an error result it reads and corrects on its next step: a hallucinated name (nothing matches), a
tool that panics, a tool's own typed `throw`, and malformed args (a `reflection.call_error` from the
boundary) alike — none of them reaches the app root. The result's `file` values are collected off the
result tree so the provider can show them to the model (their `$katari_ref` handles are already visible in
the result text)."
agent dispatch_one[effect E](tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}], call: types.tool_call) -> types.tool_result {
  // A failing tool must never fail the conversation — that is the loop's whole premise, and it holds for
  // BOTH ways a call can crash: a `panic` (a crashed handler) and any typed `throw` — the tool's own
  // `throw[my_error]` and the boundary's `throw[call_error]` for hallucinated args alike — fold back HERE
  // as an error result the model reads and corrects on its next step, instead of escalating to the app
  // root. This is why the tools' effect is spelled `{...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}`: `E` stays throw-free,
  // every error a tool can raise rides the EXPLICIT `prelude.throw[unknown]`, and the `throw(error: unknown)`
  // handler below discharges exactly that row — so `E` escalates untouched while every tool failure returns
  // to the model as feedback it can act on (a bad-arguments hallucination becomes self-correcting too).
  use handler {
    request panic(msg: string) {
      break types.tool_result(
        name = call.name,
        call_id = call.opaque,
        text = f"(tool failed: ${msg})",
        files = [],
      )
    }
  }
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: unknown) -> never {
      break types.tool_result(
        name = call.name,
        call_id = call.opaque,
        text = f"(tool failed: ${json.stringify(value = error)})",
        files = [],
      )
    }
  }
  for (let tool in tools) {
    let meta = reflection.get_metadata(value = tool)
    if (meta.name == call.name) {
      // The args are already a plain value; the boundary unconditionally turns any replayed `$katari_ref`
      // into a `file`. `stringify` renders the result's wire form (files show as `$katari_ref` handles) for
      // the model, while the actual `file` values are collected off the result tree for inlining.
      let result = reflection.call_agent(target = tool, args = call.args)
      return types.tool_result(
        name = call.name,
        call_id = call.opaque,
        text = json.stringify(value = result),
        files = collect_result_files(node = result),
      )
    } else {
      null
    }
  }
  types.tool_result(
    name = call.name,
    call_id = call.opaque,
    text = f"(error: unknown tool \"${call.name}\")",
    files = [],
  )
}

@"Collect every `file` value out of a tool result's value tree: `reflection.call_agent` returns the
tool's result as a plain value, so a `file` the tool produced (a generated image, an MCP tool's
screenshot) is an actual `file` leaf — found wherever it sits (a bare return, the MCP bridge's
{ text, files } record, or nested structure)."
agent collect_result_files(node: unknown) -> array[file] with pure {
  match (node) {
    case file(found) -> [found]
    case record(entries) -> {
      array.flatten(target = for (let value in record.values(target = entries)) {
        next collect_result_files(node = value)
      })
    }
    case array(items) -> {
      array.flatten(target = for (let item in items) {
        next collect_result_files(node = item)
      })
    }
    case _ -> []
  }
}

// ── served sessions: the serve skeleton, with token accounting and compaction ─
//
// Every chat bot hand-rolled the same skeleton: declare a request, keep the conversation in a
// handler var, run the tool loop once per message, append the reply. `serve_session` is that
// skeleton as a scoped provider — `use serve_session(...)` installs the handler, the continuation
// bridges its transport into `session_message` and (typically) never returns, a watch loop — plus
// the piece none of the hand-rolled ones had: the seam's `usage` metered into a context-occupancy
// figure, and a compaction pass when it outgrows a threshold. The policy is data (`compaction`),
// the one numeric comparison lives in `classify_budget`, and everything downstream dispatches on
// its sum. Transport specifics (a channel id, a thread ts) stay in the app's bridge agent: fold
// them into @content@ on the way in, route the returned reply on the way out.

@"How a session compacts its context once it outgrows the threshold. @threshold_tokens@ compares
against the freshest step's measured occupancy (`usage.input_tokens + output_tokens`); @prompt@
frames the summarization step; @keep_recent@ is how many trailing history elements survive
verbatim — extended forward to a clean `turn` boundary, so a call turn is never severed from the
results that answer it."
data compaction(threshold_tokens: integer, prompt: string, keep_recent: integer)

@"The default compaction policy both session shapes fall back to: compact past 100k tokens,
summarize under the standard self-note prompt, keep the last 8 history elements verbatim."
agent default_compaction() -> compaction {
  compaction(
    threshold_tokens = 100000,
    prompt = "Summarize the following conversation for your own future reference: keep every fact, decision, open question, and personal detail you might need again; drop the pleasantries and the dead ends. Answer with the summary only.",
    keep_recent = 8,
  )
}

@"The verdict on a measured context occupancy: still under the threshold, with the room left."
data within_budget(@"Tokens still free under the threshold." remaining: integer)

@"The verdict on a measured context occupancy: over the threshold — time to compact."
data over_budget(@"The measured occupancy that tripped the threshold." used: integer)

// The budget verdict. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type budget = within_budget | over_budget

@"Classify a measured context occupancy against the policy threshold — the compaction machinery's
one numeric comparison; everything downstream dispatches on the returned sum."
agent classify_budget(used: integer, policy: compaction) -> budget {
  if (used < policy.threshold_tokens) {
    within_budget(remaining = policy.threshold_tokens - used)
  } else {
    over_budget(used = used)
  }
}

@"The integer at a provider's usage field, or 0 when the field is absent — a provider that omits a
count still yields a usable measurement. Providers read their wire usage through this."
agent token_count(node: unknown) -> integer {
  match (node) {
    case integer(value) -> value
    case _ -> 0
  }
}

@"The first index at or after @start@ whose element is a plain `turn` — the clean cut for `compact`:
a `call_turn` must never be severed from the `result_turn` that answers it (a provider replays them
as a matched pair), so the kept tail always starts on a turn. No turn after @start@ means nothing
safely survives, and the whole history summarizes."
agent turn_boundary(history: array[types.message], start: integer) -> integer {
  for (let entry in array.slice(target = history, start = start, end = array.length(target = history)), var offset: integer = 0) {
    match (entry) {
      case types.turn(role => _, text => _, files => _) -> { break start + offset }
      case types.call_turn(calls => _) -> { next with { offset = offset + 1 } }
      case types.result_turn(results => _) -> { next with { offset = offset + 1 } }
    }
  } then (_entries) { array.length(target = history) }
}

@"A history element as summarizer-input text: turns as role-prefixed lines, tool traffic as compact
notes — enough for the summary to preserve what happened, nothing wire-shaped."
agent render_message(entry: types.message) -> string {
  match (entry) {
    case types.turn(role => role, text => text, files => files) -> {
      let notes = for (let attachment in files) { next f" [file ${json.stringify(value = attachment)}]" }
      let joined_notes = string.join(parts = notes, separator = "")
      f"${role}: ${text}${joined_notes}"
    }
    case types.call_turn(calls => calls) -> {
      let names = for (let call in calls) { next call.name }
      let joined_names = string.join(parts = names, separator = ", ")
      f"model: (called ${joined_names})"
    }
    case types.result_turn(results => results) -> {
      let texts = for (let result in results) { next f"${result.name} -> ${result.text}" }
      let joined_texts = string.join(parts = texts, separator = "\n")
      f"tools: ${joined_texts}"
    }
  }
}

@"Compress a conversation to fit its budget again: everything before the kept tail (the last
@policy.keep_recent@ elements, extended to a clean turn boundary) is summarized by one tool-less
model step under @policy.prompt@, and the summary replaces it as a single opening turn. A history
that is all tail comes back unchanged. Exported on its own so a hand-rolled loop can compact too —
`serve_session` is this plus the metering that decides when."
agent compact(history: array[types.message], policy: compaction) -> array[types.message] {
  let count = array.length(target = history)
  let wanted = if (count < policy.keep_recent) { 0 } else { count - policy.keep_recent }
  let cut = turn_boundary(history = history, start = wanted)
  if (cut == 0) {
    history
  } else {
    let head = array.slice(target = history, start = 0, end = cut)
    let tail = array.slice(target = history, start = cut, end = count)
    let rendered = for (let entry in head) { next render_message(entry = entry) }
    let joined = string.join(parts = rendered, separator = "\n")
    // The summarization step is a MODEL call, so it can fail like any other. A failed compaction must
    // neither lose the conversation nor crash a long-lived loop: catch the provider failure `reply`
    // re-raises and skip compaction this time (return the history unchanged); the next over-budget turn
    // tries again. This is the one place `compact` chooses to absorb `step_error` rather than propagate it.
    use handler {
      request prelude.throw(error: step_error) -> never { break history }
    }
    let summary = reply(history = [types.turn(
      role = "user",
      text = f"${policy.prompt}\n\n${joined}",
      files = [],
    )])
    array.concat(
      left = [types.turn(role = "user", text = f"[Summary of the earlier conversation]\n${summary}", files = [])],
      right = tail,
    )
  }
}

@"Raised once per incoming message of a served session; `serve_session`'s handler answers it with
the model's reply. The app's transport bridge performs it — fold any routing detail (a channel id, a
thread ts) into @content@ on the way in, route the returned reply on the way out."
request session_message(@"The incoming message, routing details folded in." content: string, @"The message's attachments." files: array[file]) -> string

@"A served session's durable state: the running conversation and the freshest measured context
occupancy — 0 until a step has run, and again right after a compaction until the next step
re-measures."
data session_state(conversation: array[types.message], used_tokens: integer)

@"One served turn's outcome: the reply, the advanced conversation, and the freshest measurement."
data session_turn(reply: string, conversation: array[types.message], used_tokens: integer)

@"The per-message tool loop of a served session — `infer_with_tools` threading the seam's usage
through: up to @max_steps@ steps, tool batches dispatched in parallel, the model's reply appended as
a model turn, and the LAST step's `input + output` carried out as the context measurement (each
step's input covers the whole context, so only the freshest figure is the occupancy — steps do not
add)."
agent session_turn_loop[effect E](
  conversation: array[types.message],
  tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  max_steps: integer,
) -> session_turn {
  let tool_metas = ensure_unique_names(metas = for (let tool in tools) { next reflection.get_metadata(value = tool) })
  for (let _step in array.range(start = 0, end = max_steps), var state: session_turn = session_turn(reply = "", conversation = conversation, used_tokens = 0)) {
    match (infer_step(history = state.conversation, tool_metas = tool_metas)) {
      // A served session is one-shot per message, so a provider failure re-raises at the perform site
      // for the app's transport bridge (the `session_message` caller) to handle.
      case inference_failed(error => error) -> { prelude.throw(error = error) }
      case inferred(result => result) -> {
        let measured = result.usage.input_tokens + result.usage.output_tokens
        match (result.step) {
          case types.step_final(text => reply_text) -> {
            break session_turn(
              reply = reply_text,
              conversation = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.turn(role = "model", text = reply_text, files = [])),
              used_tokens = measured,
            )
          }
          case types.step_call(calls => calls) -> {
            let results = parallel for (let call in calls) {
              next dispatch_one[E](tools = tools, call = call)
            }
            let with_calls = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.call_turn(calls = calls))
            next with { state = session_turn(reply = "", conversation = array.append(target = with_calls, value = types.result_turn(results = results)), used_tokens = measured) }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  } then (_steps) {
    // Budget exhausted: the shared forced tool-less close.
    let closing = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = budget_exhausted_turn())
    let answer = reply(history = closing)
    session_turn(
      reply = answer,
      conversation = array.append(target = closing, value = types.turn(role = "model", text = answer, files = [])),
      used_tokens = state.used_tokens,
    )
  }
}

@"Serve a chat session for the extent of @continuation@ — the hand-rolled serve skeleton as one
provider, with token metering and automatic compaction. The handler's var carries the conversation
across messages (durable by construction: it survives a runtime restart like any run state); each
`session_message` runs one tool loop and answers with the reply. After each turn the freshest
measured occupancy is classified against @threshold_tokens@: over it, the conversation compacts
(`compact`) under @compact_prompt@, keeping the last @keep_recent@ elements verbatim. The ambient
provider (`use gemini.provider(...)` …) and any @E@ handlers are installed by the caller, exactly as
for `infer_with_tools`; the continuation bridges its transport into `session_message` and typically
never returns (a `watch_messages` loop)."
agent serve_session[R, effect E](
  @"The conversation the model starts from (system framing, prior turns)." context: array[types.message],
  @"The tools of every turn's loop." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"The per-message step budget." max_steps: integer,
  @"The compaction policy — one `compaction(...)` datum; `null` (the default) is `default_compaction`." policy: compaction | null ?= null,
  @"The session body: bridges a transport into `session_message`." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, session_message},
) -> R {
  let effective_policy = match (policy) {
    case null -> default_compaction()
    case given -> given
  }
  use handler (var state: session_state = session_state(conversation = context, used_tokens = 0)) {
    request session_message(content: string, files: array[file]) {
      let asked = array.append(target = state.conversation, value = types.turn(role = "user", text = content, files = files))
      let outcome = session_turn_loop[E](conversation = asked, tools = tools, max_steps = max_steps)
      match (classify_budget(used = outcome.used_tokens, policy = effective_policy)) {
        case within_budget(remaining => _) -> {
          next outcome.reply with { state = session_state(conversation = outcome.conversation, used_tokens = outcome.used_tokens) }
        }
        case over_budget(used => _) -> {
          // The compacted context's occupancy is unknown until the next step measures it; 0 says
          // "not yet measured", never an estimate.
          next outcome.reply with { state = session_state(conversation = compact(history = outcome.conversation, policy = effective_policy), used_tokens = 0) }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── region-integrated loop: dynamic monitors over a nursery ───────────────────
//
// `infer_with_tools` is fork-JOIN only: every tool call is awaited before the loop moves on, so an
// agent cannot "start something and forget it". `infer_with_region` adds exactly that, over a `region`
// nursery (structured concurrency): the model may START a background monitor (a fiber that runs on its
// own and notifies the model later) and STOP it, without ever leaving the loop. A monitor speaks to the
// model through ONE channel — the `observation` request — so the nursery's effect ceiling is a single
// fixed row (`{...E, observation} | io`), and one `region.watch` covering it covers every monitor.
//
// Structured concurrency forces the shape. `region.provide` CANCELS every still-running fiber when its
// continuation returns, so for a monitor to outlive the reply that started it the loop MUST NOT return —
// it runs for the session's lifetime. Hence `infer_with_region` returns `never`. INPUT and OUTPUT are
// separate wires: the `observation` channel is fire-and-forget (a source or monitor only reports an
// event), and each turn's reply leaves through the separate `deliver_to` sink. `infer_with_tools` (the
// fork-join loop) stays for the plain request/response case; this is its long-lived sibling.

// The per-nursery scope marker for this module's region — region.ktr's "declare one marker per nursery"
// rule. A nullary phantom: `region.provide` mints it for the loop's continuation and discharges it from
// its own result, so no monitor fiber can outlive `infer_with_region`.
@"The `infer_with_region` nursery scope: a nullary phantom marker carried by every monitor fiber, minted and discharged by the loop's `region.provide` so no fiber outlives the loop."
effect ai_turn

@"THE monitor→model channel: a background monitor pushes @content@ (plus any @files@ attached to it) from
@source@ into the model's context. FIRE-AND-FORGET (`-> null`): a source or monitor only reports an event —
the model's reply is NOT handed back here but delivered on the separate `deliver_to` wire. It is the only
effect a monitor raises besides `io`, which pins the nursery's ceiling to one row so a single `region.watch`
covers every monitor. A `source` fiber or a started monitor performs it; the loop's own handler answers it
by running a model turn over the injected report. @files@ ride the injection turn's `files` slot — the same
text + files split as `types.turn` — so the provider's existing inline policy shows the model any attached image."
request observation(@"Which monitor is reporting (e.g. \"slack\"); it labels the report's injection turn." source: string, @"The report to inject into the model's context." content: string, @"Any files attached to the report (a Discord / Slack attachment), injected on the report's turn so the provider inlines them for the model." files: array[file]) -> null

@"What the model supplies when it STARTS a monitor: a free-text @topic@ naming exactly what to watch (empty
falls back to the monitor's default target), and an optional cron @schedule@ (a 5- or 6-field expression,
e.g. \"0 9 * * 1\" for every Monday at 09:00; empty means the monitor's own default cadence). `region.fork`
applies a task to `{ input: <argument> }` — one value — so the model's two flat fields ride as ONE
`instruction`, the argument the loop forks each monitor with. A monitor stays authored as
`agent (topic, schedule)` — the friendly flat schema the model sees — and the loop adapts that shape to the
fork's `agent (input: instruction)` shape with a one-line closure."
data instruction(@"Free-text description of exactly what to watch; empty falls back to the monitor's default target." topic: string, @"A cron expression (e.g. \"0 9 * * 1\"); empty falls back to the monitor's default cadence." schedule: string)

@"One entry in the started-monitor registry: the fiber @handle@ `region.cancel` needs, plus the brief the
model reads back through `list_monitors` — the monitor's @name@ and the @topic@ / @schedule@ it was
started with (each empty when the model let it fall back to the monitor's default). Keyed in the registry
by the stable id the model holds."
data monitor_entry(handle: region.fiber[ai_turn, never], name: string, topic: string, schedule: string)

@"The loop's durable state, threaded across every observation: the running @conversation@, the
@registry@ of started monitors (a stable id → the `monitor_entry` carrying the fiber handle `region.cancel`
needs and the brief `list_monitors` reads back), and the freshest measured context occupancy @used_tokens@
(0 until a step has run, and again right after a compaction until the next step re-measures). The id is
what the model holds and passes back to stop or list a monitor."
data region_session(conversation: array[types.message], registry: record[monitor_entry], used_tokens: integer)

@"One model turn's outcome: the @reply@ the loop hands to `deliver_to`, plus the advanced @session@ the
loop persists for the next observation."
data region_turn_result(reply: string, session: region_session)

@"One routed tool call: the model-facing @result@ plus the possibly-grown monitor @registry@ (a started
monitor adds its id → `monitor_entry`; a stop or a synchronous tool leaves it be)."
data region_step_result(result: types.tool_result, registry: record[monitor_entry])

@"One routed batch: the registry after the whole batch plus the results in call order — one var so the
batch fold threads both at once."
data region_batch(registry: record[monitor_entry], results: array[types.tool_result])

@"Tool: STOP a background monitor you started earlier. Pass the monitor id you were given when it
started. The region loop INTERCEPTS this call (only the loop holds the nursery `region.cancel` needs),
so this body is the schema seam and never runs; it returns a note so a stray direct call stays total."
agent cancel_monitor(@"The id you were given when the monitor started." monitor_id: string) -> string {
  f"(cancel_monitor is serviced by the region loop; id ${monitor_id})"
}

@"Tool: LIST the background monitors you currently have running — each rendered as
`id: name (topic=..., schedule=...)`, with `(default)` where you let the monitor pick its own target or
cadence, and `(no monitors running)` when none are. Call it to check what you are already watching before
starting or stopping one. The region loop INTERCEPTS this call (only the loop holds the registry), so this
body is the schema seam and never runs; it returns a note so a stray direct call stays total."
agent list_monitors() -> string {
  "(list_monitors is serviced by the region loop)"
}

@"Render the monitor registry for `list_monitors`: one `id: name (topic=..., schedule=...)` line per
running monitor (an empty topic / schedule shows as `(default)`), or `(no monitors running)` when the
registry is empty."
agent render_monitor_listing(registry: record[monitor_entry]) -> string {
  let lines = for (let [id, entry] in record.entries(target = registry)) {
    let topic_text = if (string.length(value = entry.topic) == 0) { "(default)" } else { entry.topic }
    let schedule_text = if (string.length(value = entry.schedule) == 0) { "(default)" } else { entry.schedule }
    next f"${id}: ${entry.name} (topic=${topic_text}, schedule=${schedule_text})"
  }
  if (array.length(target = lines) == 0) {
    "(no monitors running)"
  } else {
    string.join(parts = lines, separator = "\n")
  }
}

@"Route one tool call inside the region loop: STOP a monitor (`cancel_monitor` — cancel the fiber the
model's id names), LIST the running monitors (`list_monitors` — render the registry back), START a monitor
(a name in @monitors@ — `region.fork` it detached and hand the model its id), or run a SYNCHRONOUS tool
(anything else — `dispatch_one`, exactly as `infer_with_tools`). Returns the model-facing result with the
possibly-grown registry."
agent route_region_call[effect E](
  @"The nursery to fork / cancel monitors in." nursery: region.nursery[ai_turn, {...E, observation} | io],
  @"The synchronous tools (called and awaited)." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"The startable monitors (forked detached), each authored `agent (topic, schedule)`." monitors: array[agent (topic: string, schedule: string) -> never with {...E, observation} | io],
  @"The started-monitor registry (id → `monitor_entry`)." registry: record[monitor_entry],
  @"The model's tool call." call: types.tool_call,
) -> region_step_result {
  if (call.name == reflection.get_metadata(value = cancel_monitor).name) {
    // Stop: look the id up and tear its fiber down. An unknown id is fed back as an error result so the
    // model corrects; `region.cancel` is idempotent, so the entry stays (ids never collide, see below).
    let requested_id = json.text(target = json.field(target = call.args, key = "monitor_id"))
    match (record.get(target = registry, key = requested_id)) {
      case null -> region_step_result(
        result = types.tool_result(name = call.name, call_id = call.opaque, text = f"(no such monitor: ${requested_id})", files = []),
        registry = registry,
      )
      case entry -> {
        region.cancel(nursery = nursery, handle = entry.handle)
        region_step_result(
          result = types.tool_result(name = call.name, call_id = call.opaque, text = f"(monitor stopped: ${requested_id})", files = []),
          registry = registry,
        )
      }
    }
  } else if (call.name == reflection.get_metadata(value = list_monitors).name) {
    // List: the loop is the only holder of the registry, so it services `list_monitors` the same way it
    // services `cancel_monitor` — intercept the call and render the registry the model cannot otherwise see.
    region_step_result(
      result = types.tool_result(name = call.name, call_id = call.opaque, text = render_monitor_listing(registry = registry), files = []),
      registry = registry,
    )
  } else {
    // Start the monitor whose name the model picked, else fall through to a synchronous tool.
    // `region.fork` returns AT ONCE with the fiber handle; the fiber runs on and notifies through
    // `observation`. The id is derived from the registry size and entries are never removed, so ids
    // grow monotonically and never collide.
    for (let monitor in monitors, var registry_acc: record[monitor_entry] = registry) {
      if (reflection.get_metadata(value = monitor).name == call.name) {
        let id = f"monitor-${string.to_string(value = record.size(target = registry_acc) + 1)}"
        // The model's args are the monitor's flat `{ topic, schedule }`; a field the model omitted degrades
        // to the empty string (the monitor reads that as "use my default"), so a bare start still works.
        let topic = json.text(target = json.field(target = call.args, key = "topic"))
        let schedule = json.text(target = json.field(target = call.args, key = "schedule"))
        // The monitor's name, captured now so the fork adapter's failure notice can name which monitor died,
        // and so the registry entry `list_monitors` reads back can label the fiber.
        let monitor_name = reflection.get_metadata(value = monitor).name
        // `region.fork` applies its task to `{ input: <argument> }` — one value — so wrap the two fields as
        // one `instruction` and adapt the monitor's `(topic, schedule)` shape to the fork's `(input:)` shape.
        // A monitor fiber that CRASHES (a panic) otherwise tears down silently — the model started a watcher
        // and would never learn it died (worse, the panic wells up through `region.watch` and takes the whole
        // loop with it). Catch it inside the fiber, tell the model through the SAME `observation` channel every
        // monitor reports on (so it can restart the watcher if it still wants it), then idle forever: a monitor
        // is `never`, so after notifying there is nothing to return to, and the model reclaims the dead fiber
        // with `cancel_monitor`. (A typed `throw` cannot be folded back the same way — a `throw` handler's
        // overwrite semantics require the fiber's abstract effect generic to exclude `throw`, which it cannot,
        // so a monitor's typed error still escalates through `region.watch` as before; only `panic` is ambient
        // enough to catch here. See dispatch_one for the same asymmetry on the synchronous tool path.)
        agent start(input: instruction) -> never {
          use handler {
            request panic(msg: string) {
              observation(source = "monitor", content = f"(monitor ${monitor_name} failed: ${msg}. It has stopped; start it again if still needed.)", files = [])
              forever { time.sleep(milliseconds = 3600000.0) }
            }
          }
          monitor(topic = input.topic, schedule = input.schedule)
        }
        let handle = region.fork(nursery = nursery, task = start, argument = instruction(topic = topic, schedule = schedule))
        break region_step_result(
          result = types.tool_result(name = call.name, call_id = call.opaque, text = f"(monitor started with id ${id}; it will notify you as observations)", files = []),
          registry = record.set(target = registry_acc, key = id, value = monitor_entry(handle = handle, name = monitor_name, topic = topic, schedule = schedule)),
        )
      } else {
        next with { registry_acc = registry_acc }
      }
    } then (_monitors) {
      region_step_result(result = dispatch_one[E](tools = tools, call = call), registry = registry_acc)
    }
  }
}

@"A turn the loop had to ABANDON because the provider surfaced a failure (`inference_failed`) mid-turn.
Rather than let that failure unwind the long-lived loop, the region loop folds it into a turn result: a
short notice for `deliver_to` (so the channel sees it) plus a `[model step failed …]` user turn appended to
the persisted conversation (so the model learns, next observation, that the turn was dropped and its
context kept). The completed call / result exchanges earlier in the turn stay in the conversation, and the
last measured occupancy carries forward unchanged."
agent abandoned_turn(conversation: array[types.message], registry: record[monitor_entry], used_tokens: integer, error: step_error) -> region_turn_result {
  let rendered = json.stringify(value = error)
  region_turn_result(
    reply = f"(I hit a model error and had to drop that turn: ${rendered})",
    session = region_session(
      conversation = array.append(
        target = conversation,
        value = types.turn(role = "user", text = f"[model step failed: ${rendered}. The turn was abandoned; context is preserved.]", files = []),
      ),
      registry = registry,
      used_tokens = used_tokens,
    ),
  )
}

@"One model turn-loop over @conversation@ WITH region powers — the region-aware twin of the
`infer_with_tools` loop. Up to @max_steps@ times: ask the provider for a step; on a tool batch, route
every call (`route_region_call`: a synchronous tool feeds its result back, a monitor tool forks a
detached fiber whose id feeds back, `cancel_monitor` stops one, `list_monitors` reports them), record the
call + result turns, and continue; on a final answer, stop. A provider FAILURE does not unwind the loop —
it is ABSORBED (`abandoned_turn`): the turn is dropped, a notice delivered, and the loop awaits the next
observation. Budget spent, force one tool-less close (as `infer_with_tools`, absorbing a failure there
too). Returns the reply plus the advanced session (conversation + monitor registry)."
agent region_turn_loop[effect E](
  @"The nursery to fork / cancel monitors in." nursery: region.nursery[ai_turn, {...E, observation} | io],
  @"The conversation to advance (the observation already injected)." conversation: array[types.message],
  @"The synchronous tools." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"The startable monitors, each authored `agent (topic, schedule)`." monitors: array[agent (topic: string, schedule: string) -> never with {...E, observation} | io],
  @"The started-monitor registry." registry: record[monitor_entry],
  @"The per-turn step budget." max_steps: integer,
) -> region_turn_result {
  let sync_metas = for (let tool in tools) { next reflection.get_metadata(value = tool) }
  let monitor_metas = for (let monitor in monitors) { next reflection.get_metadata(value = monitor) }
  // The model sees every synchronous tool, every startable monitor, and the two monitor-control tools
  // (`cancel_monitor` to stop one, `list_monitors` to see what is running).
  let control_metas = [reflection.get_metadata(value = cancel_monitor), reflection.get_metadata(value = list_monitors)]
  let tool_metas = ensure_unique_names(metas = array.concat(
    left = array.concat(left = sync_metas, right = monitor_metas),
    right = control_metas,
  ))
  for (let _step in array.range(start = 0, end = max_steps), var session: region_session = region_session(conversation = conversation, registry = registry, used_tokens = 0)) {
    match (infer_step(history = session.conversation, tool_metas = tool_metas)) {
      // ABSORB a provider failure: abandon just this turn (notice out, marker in) and wait for the next
      // observation — the long-lived loop must survive a step failure, not unwind on it.
      case inference_failed(error => error) -> {
        break abandoned_turn(conversation = session.conversation, registry = session.registry, used_tokens = session.used_tokens, error = error)
      }
      case inferred(result => result) -> {
        // Each step's input covers the whole context, so the FRESHEST figure IS the occupancy (steps do not add).
        let measured = result.usage.input_tokens + result.usage.output_tokens
        match (result.step) {
          case types.step_final(text => reply_text) -> {
            // Record the model's reply as a model turn — exactly as `session_turn_loop` does — so the running
            // conversation is a faithful transcript the next observation (and `compact`) can read back.
            break region_turn_result(
              reply = reply_text,
              session = region_session(
                conversation = array.append(target = session.conversation, value = types.turn(role = "model", text = reply_text, files = [])),
                registry = session.registry,
                used_tokens = measured,
              ),
            )
          }
          case types.step_call(calls => calls) -> {
            // Route the batch SEQUENTIALLY: starting / stopping a monitor threads the durable registry, so —
            // unlike `infer_with_tools`' parallel batch — the calls run in order (synchronous tools included).
            let batch = for (let call in calls, var acc: region_batch = region_batch(registry = session.registry, results = [])) {
              let step = route_region_call[E](nursery = nursery, tools = tools, monitors = monitors, registry = acc.registry, call = call)
              next with { acc = region_batch(registry = step.registry, results = array.append(target = acc.results, value = step.result)) }
            } then (_calls) { acc }
            let with_calls = array.append(target = session.conversation, value = types.call_turn(calls = calls))
            let with_results = array.append(target = with_calls, value = types.result_turn(results = batch.results))
            next with { session = region_session(conversation = with_results, registry = batch.registry, used_tokens = measured) }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  } then (_steps) {
    // Budget exhausted: force ONE tool-less step so the model commits to an answer instead of losing
    // everything the tools gathered (the same close as `infer_with_tools`) — but drive the seam directly so
    // a failure here is ABSORBED too, keeping the region loop total.
    let closing = array.append(target = session.conversation, value = budget_exhausted_turn())
    match (infer_step(history = closing, tool_metas = [])) {
      case inference_failed(error => error) -> {
        abandoned_turn(conversation = closing, registry = session.registry, used_tokens = session.used_tokens, error = error)
      }
      case inferred(result => result) -> {
        let answer = match (result.step) {
          case types.step_final(text => text) -> text
          case types.step_call(calls => _) -> "(unexpected tool call without tools)"
        }
        region_turn_result(
          reply = answer,
          session = region_session(
            conversation = array.append(target = closing, value = types.turn(role = "model", text = answer, files = [])),
            registry = session.registry,
            used_tokens = result.usage.input_tokens + result.usage.output_tokens,
          ),
        )
      }
    }
  }
}

@"Run the tool-using model loop over a `region` nursery, so the model can START background monitors and
STOP them — the long-lived sibling of `infer_with_tools`. @tools@ are synchronous (called and awaited,
exactly as `infer_with_tools`); @monitors@ are startable background fibers the model launches as tools
(each forks detached and reports through `observation`) and stops with `cancel_monitor`;
@sources@ are always-on input fibers forked at startup (e.g. a chat watcher that turns each user message
into an `observation`) and are NOT shown as tools. @context@ seeds the conversation the model always
sees, and @region_instructions@ (overridable) teaches the model the start / stop protocol, since a model
is trained on tool calls but not on this fork / cancel discipline.

INPUT and OUTPUT are separate wires. `observation` is fire-and-forget — a source or monitor only reports an
event — and each turn's reply is delivered by @deliver_to@, the ONE place output is wired; a source /
monitor never sends a reply.

Returns `never`: structured concurrency cancels every fiber when the nursery closes, so to keep monitors
alive the loop never returns — it runs until the run is cancelled. The provider (`use gemini.provider(...)`
…) and any @E@ handlers are installed by the caller, exactly as for `infer_with_tools`.

COMPACTION: unlike the plain loop, the conversation never resets across observations, so after each turn
the freshest measured occupancy is classified against @threshold_tokens@ and, over it, the conversation is
summarized back down (`compact`, the same policy as `serve_session`)."
agent infer_with_region[effect E](
  @"The conversation the model always sees (system framing, prior turns)." context: array[types.message],
  @"Synchronous tools: called and awaited, the result fed back." tools: array[agent never -> unknown with {...E, prelude.throw[unknown]}],
  @"Startable monitors, each authored `agent (topic, schedule)`: forked detached when the model calls one (with the model's topic + schedule), they notify through `observation`." monitors: array[agent (topic: string, schedule: string) -> never with {...E, observation} | io],
  @"Always-on input fibers forked at startup (not shown as tools)." sources: array[agent (input: null) -> never with {...E, observation} | io],
  @"The ONE output wire: called once per observation with the model's reply @text@ and any @files@ on it. Established like `discord.watch_messages`' `deliver_to` — the app supplies it, and its effects flow to the app's handlers." deliver_to: agent (text: string, files: array[file]) -> null with {...E, observation} | io,
  @"The per-observation step budget." max_steps: integer,
  @"How the model should use the monitor start / stop tools; override to specialise." region_instructions: string ?= "You have background MONITOR tools in addition to ordinary tools. An ordinary tool returns its result immediately. Calling a MONITOR tool instead STARTS a background watcher and returns a monitor id; that watcher keeps running on its own and notifies you later, each notification arriving as a 'Notification from <source>' message. When you start a monitor you may give it a topic (free text describing exactly what to watch) and a schedule (a cron expression, e.g. '0 9 * * 1' = every Monday at 09:00; leave it empty to use the monitor's default cadence); leave the topic empty to watch its default target. To STOP a monitor, call cancel_monitor with the id you were given. To SEE what monitors you currently have running, call list_monitors — it returns each running monitor's id, name, topic and schedule, so you never have to guess an id. Start a monitor when the user asks you to keep an eye on something, and stop it when they ask you to stop. If a monitor fails it stops and sends you a 'Notification from monitor' saying so; start it again if you still need that watch.",
  @"The compaction policy — one `compaction(...)` datum; `null` (the default) is `default_compaction`." policy: compaction | null ?= null,
) -> never {
  // A region session never returns, so its conversation would grow without bound; the same compaction
  // policy `serve_session` uses folds it back under the threshold after any over-budget turn.
  let effective_policy = match (policy) {
    case null -> default_compaction()
    case given -> given
  }
  let nursery: region.nursery[ai_turn, {...E, observation} | io] = use region.provide[ai_turn, {...E, observation} | io]
  // The durable state (conversation + monitor registry + freshest occupancy) lives in the handler that
  // answers every observation, so every model turn — from a source or from a started monitor — shares one thread.
  use handler (var session: region_session = region_session(
    conversation = array.concat(
      left = [types.turn(role = "user", text = region_instructions, files = [])],
      right = context,
    ),
    registry = record.empty(),
    used_tokens = 0,
  )) {
    request observation(source: string, content: string, files: array[file]) {
      // The report's files ride the injection turn's `files` slot, exactly as an ordinary user turn's
      // attachments — so the provider's existing inline policy (`gemini_file_parts` /
      // `anthropic_turn_content`) shows the model any attached image with no observation-specific path.
      let injected = array.append(
        target = session.conversation,
        value = types.turn(role = "user", text = f"Notification from ${source}:\n${content}", files = files),
      )
      let outcome = region_turn_loop[E](
        nursery = nursery,
        conversation = injected,
        tools = tools,
        monitors = monitors,
        registry = session.registry,
        max_steps = max_steps,
      )
      // Deliver this turn's reply on the separate output wire. The model's final text carries no files of
      // its own, so the `files` slot is empty; `observation` itself resolves with `null` (fire-and-forget).
      deliver_to(text = outcome.reply, files = [])
      // Classify the freshest measured occupancy against the policy, exactly as `serve_session` does: an
      // over-budget turn summarizes everything before the kept tail. (0 says "not yet measured" after a
      // compaction — the next step re-measures.)
      match (classify_budget(used = outcome.session.used_tokens, policy = effective_policy)) {
        case within_budget(remaining => _) -> {
          next null with { session = outcome.session }
        }
        case over_budget(used => _) -> {
          next null with { session = region_session(conversation = compact(history = outcome.session.conversation, policy = effective_policy), registry = outcome.session.registry, used_tokens = 0) }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Start the always-on inputs, then pump every fiber's observations up to the handler above forever.
  let _sources = for (let source in sources) { next region.fork(nursery = nursery, task = source, argument = null) }
  region.watch(nursery = nursery)
}

// ── ambient-context middleware ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@"Middleware over the seam: EVERY `infer_step`, prepend @inject@'s freshly computed text as a user
turn and re-perform outward to the ambient provider — the generalization of the bot-side pattern
that re-injects the current time and a memory index each turn. Install it INSIDE the provider
(`use gemini.provider(...)` then `use ai.with_context(inject = ...)`), so the re-performed step
reaches the model. An EMPTY injection skips the extra turn; the injected turn is never part of the
conversation a loop persists (it is derived fresh each step, so persisting it would double it).
`infer_object` passes through un-injected — structured extraction states its own context."
agent with_context[R, effect E](
  @"Computes the note to prepend this step — ambient state (the time, a memory index), fresh each step." inject: agent (value: null) -> string with {...E, infer_step},
  @"The program to run with the injection installed." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, infer_step},
) -> R with E | infer_step {
  use handler {
    request infer_step(history: array[types.message], tool_metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> step_outcome {
      let note = inject(value = null)
      let injected = if (note == "") {
        history
      } else {
        array.concat(left = [types.turn(role = "user", text = note, files = [])], right = history)
      }
      // Re-performing escalates PAST this handler to the ambient provider — the outward re-perform
      // semantics a rethrown request uses.
      next infer_step(history = injected, tool_metas = tool_metas)
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── wire tool names ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Katari enforces namespaces, so a tool's derived name is ALWAYS qualified (`katari_verify.count_letters`).
// A provider's name vocabulary is narrower than that: Anthropic and OpenAI validate every tool name —
// on the request's tool declarations AND on the tool_use / tool_call blocks replayed in history —
// against `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$`, which forbids the dot (a qualified name 400s). So a provider whose
// vocabulary is too narrow shapes the qualified name down to a wire name here, and reverses the model's
// pick back to the real name before the loop dispatches it (the loop resolves a call by matching
// `tool_call.name` against tool metadata, so that name MUST stay the real qualified one). Gemini's
// vocabulary already admits the dot, so its provider never consults this — a provider only maps when
// its wire demands it.
//
// The map is a pure function of the step's `tool_metas` (their order included), so every step rebuilds
// the identical map from the same tools and no durable state is needed to keep request and response
// agreeing on a name.

@"A deterministic two-way mapping between a tool's real (qualified) name and the shortened name a
provider sends on the wire. @real_to_wire@ is keyed by the qualified name (used when encoding a tool
declaration or replaying a call turn), @wire_to_real@ by the wire name (used to reverse the model's
pick); both are total over one step's tools — see @build_name_map@."
data name_map(
  @"Qualified tool name → wire name." real_to_wire: record[string],
  @"Wire name → qualified tool name." wire_to_real: record[string],
)

@"Fold a wire name onto a qualified name: every character outside a provider's `[a-zA-Z0-9_-]` name
alphabet (the dot of a qualified name, above all) folds to `_`, then the result is capped at the
providers' 64-character limit."
agent sanitize_name(@"The qualified tool name." name: string) -> string {
  let allowed = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789_-"
  let mapped = for (let character in string.split(value = name, separator = "")) {
    if (string.contains(value = allowed, search = character)) {
      next character
    } else {
      next "_"
    }
  }
  string.slice(value = string.join(parts = mapped, separator = ""), start = 0, end = 64)
}

@"Disambiguate a colliding wire name with an ordinal suffix (`_2`, `_3`, …), reserving room so the
result still fits the 64-character cap."
agent suffixed_name(@"The colliding base wire name." base: string, @"The 1-based ordinal." index: integer) -> string {
  let suffix = f"_${string.to_string(value = index)}"
  let room = 64 - string.length(value = suffix)
  f"${string.slice(value = base, start = 0, end = room)}${suffix}"
}

@"Choose a free wire name for @real@ given the wire names already @taken@: its sanitized form, or —
when sanitizing collapsed two distinct qualified names onto one token, or truncation shared a prefix —
the first free ordinal-suffixed form. The suffix is decided by @taken@'s size (processing order), so
the choice is deterministic."
agent assign_wire_name(@"The wire names already assigned this step." taken: record[string], @"The qualified name to place." real: string) -> string {
  let base = sanitize_name(name = real)
  let count = record.size(target = taken)
  // Indices 1..count+1 yield count+1 DISTINCT candidates (base, then `base_2`, `base_3`, …); at most
  // `count` names are taken, so at least one candidate is free and the loop always breaks. `chosen`
  // stays `base` only in the unreachable no-break case.
  for (let index in array.range(start = 1, end = count + 2), var chosen: string = base) {
    let candidate = if (index == 1) { base } else { suffixed_name(base = base, index = index) }
    if (record.has(target = taken, key = candidate)) {
      next with { chosen = chosen }
    } else {
      break candidate
    }
  } then (_indices) { chosen }
}

@"Build the deterministic wire-name @name_map@ for one step's tools. Walking @tool_metas@ in order,
each tool's qualified name is assigned a free wire name against the names already placed, so the same
tool list always yields the same map — which is why the map needs no durable state (see the section
note). A provider whose wire vocabulary admits the qualified name (Gemini) never calls this."
agent build_name_map(@"The step's tools, in order." tool_metas: array[reflection.agent_metadata]) -> name_map {
  for (let meta in tool_metas, var acc: name_map = name_map(real_to_wire = record.empty(), wire_to_real = record.empty())) {
    let wire = assign_wire_name(taken = acc.wire_to_real, real = meta.name)
    next with {
      acc = name_map(
        real_to_wire = record.set(target = acc.real_to_wire, key = meta.name, value = wire),
        wire_to_real = record.set(target = acc.wire_to_real, key = wire, value = meta.name),
      )
    }
  } then (_metas) { acc }
}

@"The wire name for a qualified tool name — used to encode a tool declaration and to re-encode a
replayed call turn. Falls back to the qualified name for a tool absent from the map (never happens for
a map built from the same step's tools)."
agent wire_name_of(@"The step's name map." map: name_map, @"The qualified tool name." real: string) -> string {
  match (record.get(target = map.real_to_wire, key = real)) {
    case null -> real
    case wire -> wire
  }
}

@"The qualified tool name for a wire name — used to reverse the model's pick so the loop dispatches by
the real name. Falls back to the wire name itself for an unknown name (a hallucinated tool), which the
loop then reports as an unknown tool."
agent real_name_of(@"The step's name map." map: name_map, @"The wire name the model returned." wire: string) -> string {
  match (record.get(target = map.wire_to_real, key = wire)) {
    case null -> wire
    case real -> real
  }
}
